1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,370
Aarav touches the two
probes of a meter together.

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:05,975
The needle jumps all the way to the right.

3
00:00:06,325 --> 00:00:08,302
And the scale there says zero.

4
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:11,243
Zero ohms on the right,
infinity on the left.

5
00:00:11,243 --> 00:00:13,070
Why is this scale backwards?

6
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:15,628
An ohmmeter measures resistance.

7
00:00:15,628 --> 00:00:18,819
But a resistor cannot push
any current by itself.

8
00:00:19,169 --> 00:00:22,163
So the ohmmeter carries
its own battery inside.

9
00:00:22,514 --> 00:00:25,124
The simplest one is the series ohmmeter.

10
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:30,150
A cell, a galvanometer and a resistor,
all in series, ending in two probes.

11
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,715
Let's run it on the breadboard.

12
00:00:33,065 --> 00:00:35,289
First, short the probes together.

13
00:00:35,289 --> 00:00:39,671
Turn the zero adjust knob until the
needle sits exactly at full scale.

14
00:00:40,021 --> 00:00:41,699
That mark is zero ohms.

15
00:00:41,699 --> 00:00:44,206
The current is as big as it can be.

16
00:00:44,856 --> 00:00:46,690
Now pull the probes apart.

17
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:50,417
No current flows, so the
needle falls back to the left.

18
00:00:50,417 --> 00:00:51,994
That mark is infinity.

19
00:00:52,644 --> 00:00:55,250
Put an unknown resistor
between the probes.

20
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:58,994
Less current flows, and the
needle stops somewhere in between.

21
00:00:59,344 --> 00:01:00,499
Watch the scope.

22
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:03,195
A bigger resistance
gives a smaller current.

23
00:01:03,195 --> 00:01:05,563
That is why the scale runs backwards.

24
00:01:06,213 --> 00:01:08,031
So where does each mark go?

25
00:01:08,031 --> 00:01:09,274
Let's work it out.

26
00:01:09,624 --> 00:01:12,644
Call the ohmmeter's own
total resistance R in.

27
00:01:12,644 --> 00:01:17,345
With the probes shorted, the full
scale current is E divided by R in.

28
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:23,812
With an unknown resistor R x, the
current is E divided by R in plus R x.

29
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:25,827
Divide the two.

30
00:01:25,827 --> 00:01:31,132
The needle's fraction of full scale
is R in, divided by R in plus R x.

31
00:01:31,832 --> 00:01:36,244
When R x equals R in, the needle
stops exactly at half scale.

32
00:01:36,944 --> 00:01:39,564
And doubling R x does
not halve the needle.

33
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:41,997
So the marks crowd together on the left.

34
00:01:42,647 --> 00:01:43,803
Worked example.

35
00:01:43,803 --> 00:01:49,023
An ohmmeter has a one point five volt cell
and a meter that reads one milliampere

36
00:01:49,023 --> 00:01:50,189
at full scale.

37
00:01:50,539 --> 00:01:55,668
R in equals one point five divided
by one times ten to the minus three.

38
00:01:55,668 --> 00:01:57,851
That is fifteen hundred ohms.

39
00:01:58,451 --> 00:02:01,666
So the half scale mark
reads fifteen hundred ohms.

40
00:02:02,316 --> 00:02:04,506
Now connect three thousand ohms.

41
00:02:04,506 --> 00:02:08,998
The fraction is fifteen hundred
divided by four thousand five hundred.

42
00:02:09,598 --> 00:02:11,541
That is one third of full scale.

43
00:02:12,191 --> 00:02:14,986
Why do we need the zero
adjust knob at all?

44
00:02:15,336 --> 00:02:19,807
As the cell gets weaker, E falls,
and the needle no longer reaches full

45
00:02:19,807 --> 00:02:20,707
scale.

46
00:02:20,395 --> 00:02:23,874
The knob brings it back,
so zero ohms stays at zero.

47
00:02:24,524 --> 00:02:28,698
For very small resistances, a shunt
type ohmmeter puts the resistor in

48
00:02:28,698 --> 00:02:30,752
parallel with the meter instead.

49
00:02:31,102 --> 00:02:34,839
Its scale runs the normal way,
with zero ohms on the left.

50
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:37,629
Two rules keep the reading honest.

51
00:02:37,979 --> 00:02:41,520
Never measure resistance in a
circuit that is switched on.

52
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:45,354
Its own voltage spoils the
reading, and can damage the meter.

53
00:02:45,704 --> 00:02:50,023
And take the part out, or lift one
end, so other parts connected across

54
00:02:50,023 --> 00:02:51,941
it do not change the reading.

55
00:02:52,591 --> 00:02:55,306
An ohmmeter does not
measure ohms directly.

56
00:02:55,306 --> 00:02:58,223
It measures current,
and turns it into ohms.

57
00:02:59,023 --> 00:03:00,489
Now, the exam trap.

58
00:03:00,839 --> 00:03:04,829
Many students think the ohmmeter
scale is even, like an ammeter's.

59
00:03:05,179 --> 00:03:06,152
It is not.

60
00:03:06,152 --> 00:03:08,570
The scale is reversed and crowded.

61
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:12,735
Half scale means R x equals
R in, not half of some range.

62
00:03:13,385 --> 00:03:14,889
Where is this asked?

63
00:03:14,889 --> 00:03:17,584
In PUC board papers, as short answers.

64
00:03:17,584 --> 00:03:22,095
What an ohmmeter is, why it needs
zero adjustment, and the precautions

65
00:03:22,095 --> 00:03:23,286
while using it.

66
00:03:23,936 --> 00:03:24,836
Try this.

67
00:03:24,798 --> 00:03:29,626
An ohmmeter uses a three volt cell and
a meter of one hundred microamperes

68
00:03:29,626 --> 00:03:30,593
full scale.

69
00:03:30,593 --> 00:03:35,084
Which resistance makes the needle
stop at one fifth of full scale?

70
00:03:40,484 --> 00:03:41,384
C.

71
00:03:40,946 --> 00:03:43,489
One hundred and twenty kilo ohms.

72
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:48,834
First, R in is three divided by one
hundred times ten to the minus six.

73
00:03:48,834 --> 00:03:50,707
That is thirty kilo ohms.

74
00:03:51,057 --> 00:03:56,549
One fifth of full scale means R in,
over R in plus R x, equals one fifth.

75
00:03:56,899 --> 00:03:59,942
So R in plus R x is five times R in.

76
00:03:59,942 --> 00:04:04,455
R x is four times thirty, one
hundred and twenty kilo ohms.

77
00:04:05,105 --> 00:04:06,626
Screenshot this card.

78
00:04:09,826 --> 00:04:12,610
Next, analogue and digital multimeters.

79
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:15,961
One box that measures
volts, amperes and ohms.
